        "titulo": "Personal pronouns change like nouns",
        "explicacao": "STEP 1: in English, \"I\" becomes \"me\" and \"my\"; in Russian, pronouns also change according to their function, in the same SIX cases as nouns. STEP 2: the nominative is the one you already know (я, ты, он, она, мы, вы, они). STEP 3: the ACCUSATIVE and the GENITIVE have the same form in pronouns: «меня, тебя, его, её, нас, вас, их». STEP 4: the DATIVE answers «кому?»: «мне, тебе, ему, ей, нам, вам, им». STEP 5: the INSTRUMENTAL answers «с кем?»: «мной, тобой, им, ей, нами, вами, ими». STEP 6: the PREPOSITIONAL always comes after a preposition («о», «в», «на»): «обо мне, о тебе, о нём, о ней, о нас, о вас, о них». STEP 7: «его» is pronounced yeh-VOH and «её» yeh-YOH: the «г» of «его» sounds like a v.",
        "comparacao": "English has different forms for \"I\", \"me\", \"my\" and \"mine\". Russian does the same thing with a single mechanism: the case. Learn the table once and use it with any verb or preposition.",
